    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d605">

                <pb facs="0784" n="690" sameAs="frus:frus1896/pg690"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Olney to Mr.
                            Taylor.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1896-10-23"><hi rendition="#italic">October 23,
                        1896</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 595.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the Department’s instruction
                    No. 589 of the 15th instant, in which papers relating to certain tobacco owned
                    by Louis Wertheimer, in Habana, were forwarded to you with instruction to urge
                    upon the Spanish Government the right of Mr. Wertheimer to ship this tobacco to
                    the United States, I inclose copy of a letter<anchor n="1" xml:id="d605-f1"/>
                    dated the 14th instant, from Mr. H. D. Winton, attorney for Mr. Wertheimer, and
                    along with it additional evidence in support of the latter’s claim that the
                    tobacco was purchased and paid for prior to the promulgation of Captain-General
                    Weyler’s decree. Being green, it was impossible to ship it within the ten days’
                    grace allowed by that decree. This tobacco is clearly exempt from the Cuban
                    embargo under the admission of the Spanish Government telegraphed by Mr.
                    Armstrong May 25, 1896, viz, that all contracts for Cuban leaf tobacco entered
                    into before the publication of the Captain-General’s decree would be respected.
                    You are requested to impress upon the Spanish minister for foreign affairs <pb
                        facs="0785" n="691" xml:id="pg691"/> the importance of immediate attention
                    to this matter. Mr. Wertheimer’s business is suffering on account of this
                    unlawful interference.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Richard Olney.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
